  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Selasar Sunaryo</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Open Arms is a project that aims at encouraging inclusivity in the field of visual art through
exhibition and its educational programs. It responds to some problems which indicate that
exclusivism and discrimination have been rooted too deeply in the Indonesian art world.
These days, visiting visual art exhibitions, festivals and biennales have become part of an increasingly
popular urban lifestyle. However, the very low percentage of disabled audiences in the visitor
population indicates that art exhibition has not yet become a proper, inclusive educational public
space. Educational programs such as exhibition guided tour, workshop, artist talk, etc.still lack of
innovation and have not given priority to people with disabilities as part of public who are also
entitled to education through arts.
In the last ten years, some initiatives that promote arts with disabilities have emerged in various
cities in Indonesia. Disabled artists have formed groups/communities, and managed to organize their
own exhibitions and activities. Nevertheless, most of their exhibition still tend to be seen as
different, even secluded event from the mainstream art world&#x2019;s activities in general. Opportunities
for disabled artists to be able to join curated projects/exhibitions in major museums and galleries
are still rare.Art organizers and institutions have not also provided sufficient job opportunities for
disabled people to work in the arts (e.g. as administrators, curators, critics, educators, handlers,
etc.). All in all, the current situation indicates that the awareness of inclusivity among Indonesian art
institutions, curators, art educators, exhibition guides, gallerist or art organizers, in general, are still
relatively low. Cooperation networks amongst art institution/organization to promote inclusivity in
arts is still absent.
Objectives
- To encourage cooperation amongst Indonesian art institutions, museums, galleries,
professional festival/exhibition/biennale organizers to to promote inclusivity in the arts by
initiating more inclusive art programs and providing better job opportunities for people with
disabilities to work in the arts.
- To engage people with disabilities in multi-faceted training and artistic activities that
lessening thegap between the disabled and non-disabled people in the art world.</narrative>
  </description>
